ntioned a previous slide, I'll assume Slide 1 is the first one, so \\\"cover\\\" is correct.\\n\\nSlide 2: \\\"Ứng dụng phổ biến của giao diện Midnight\\\" — applications. The content lists where it's applied. This looks like a typical bullet points slide, so \\\"bullets\\\" would be suitable. The user's example uses \\\"bullets\\\" for slide_index 1, which aligns with this.\\n\\nSlide 3: \\\"Lợi ích ban đầu của giao diện Midnight\\\" — benefits. The bullets here are about advantages, so \\\"pros-cons\\\" might be a possibility, but since there are only two points and it's just benefits, \\\"bullets\\\" is more straightforward. \\\"Pros-cons\\\" is for comparing pros and cons, but here it's just listing benefits. So \\\"bullets\\\" again.\\n\\nWait, but the third slide's content is about benefits. Maybe \\\"pros-cons\\\" isn't right. Let me check the layout definitions. \\\"Pros-cons\\\" is for two columns of pros and cons. Since this slide only has benefits, not a comparison, \\\"bullets\\\" is better. So all three slides would be cover, bullets, bullets? That seems possible. Alternatively, if Slide 3 is a list of benefits, maybe \\\"pros-cons\\\" could still be used if the points are split into two columns. But the user's content here is just two bullet points, so \\\"bullets\\\" is more appropriate.\\n\\nAnother thought: \\\"section-header\\\" is for when you have a section title and then a series of slides under that section. If Slide 2 and 3 are subsections under a main section, maybe Slide 1 should be a section-header. But since the user hasn't indicated any sections, perhaps the first slide is the cover. \\n\\nSo final answer: Slide 1 (index 0) is \\\"cover\\\", Slide 2 (index 1) is \\\"bullets\\\", Slide 3 (index 2) is \\\"bullets\\\".\\n</think>\\n\\n[{\\\"slide_index\\\": 0, \\\"layout\\\": \\\"cover\\\"}, {\\\"slide_index\\\": 1, \\\"layout\\\": \\\"bullets\\\"}, {\\\"slide_index\\\": 2, \\\"layout\\\": \\\"bullets\\\"}]\",\"generationInfo\":{\"finish_rea